.netscript/docs/pages/durable-workflows/sagas/index.md:11:This is the third capability in the continuous-app thread. The [background jobs](https://rickylabs.github.io/netscript/background-processing/workers/) capability ended with a job, `create-user-settings`, that publishes a `UserSettingsCreated` message. Here that message stops being fire-and-forget: a saga **consumes it**, advances its own state, and emits a `sagaComplete(...)` effect that the runtime records as a first-class outcome — and persists durably so the instance survives a process restart.
.netscript/docs/pages/durable-workflows/sagas/index.md:71:[Do — Wire a durable workflow  Add the sagas plugin to a running app, consume a cross-plugin message, and emit sagaComplete end to end.](https://rickylabs.github.io/netscript/netscript/tutorials/storefront/04-checkout-saga/)
.netscript/docs/pages/durable-workflows/sagas/index.md:83:Every handler returns an array of effects — sagaComplete, sagaFail, sagaCompensate, send, and schedule are named outcomes. spawn is exported only to fail immediately with SAGA_NOT_IMPLEMENTED.
.netscript/docs/pages/durable-workflows/sagas/index.md:99:The runnable shape you grow into: a real state type, multiple `.on(...)` handlers, and a `.compensate(...)` branch that unwinds an already-charged payment when fulfillment fails. The saga records workflow state and consumes results published by explicit worker boundaries. It does not use `send(...)` to address a service or job. The failure path returns `sagaCompensate(...)` so the engine routes the matching compensation handler, records cancellation, and then fails the instance.
